-- Library module A of the DOUBLE-collision witness: declares `Thing` (short
-- name shared with DcLibB.Thing) AND a constructor named `Shared` (also shared
-- with DcLibB.Thing's `Shared`) — a "double collision" (same short type name
-- AND a shared ctor name). This is the shape the interim Task-6b stopgap
-- rejected; the constructor module-qualified identity plan makes it dispatch
-- correctly. `say` constructs via the SHARED ctor `Shared` and calls `speak` on
-- it from INSIDE this module — the interpreter dispatches a nested/imported-
-- module interface method only when the call originates in the impl's own module
-- (a pre-existing scoping limitation, same as sc_lib_a.march), so the cross-
-- backend witness triggers dispatch this way. Correct dispatch must route this
-- module's `Shared` value to module A's own impl body ("from-A").
mod DcLibA do
  type Thing = Shared | OnlyA
  impl Speak(Thing) do
    fn speak(_self) do "from-A" end
  end
  fn say() do speak(Shared) end
end
